Show native views on the web: the app's DOM element under GPUI's canvas, its hole cut by the wgpu renderer (0047).

What:
- gpui_web: `NativeView::new` takes a `web_sys::HtmlElement`. GPUI moves it into a slot of its own, in a layer before
  the canvas (the canvas is raised above it, `position: relative; z-index: 1`, while views exist; the layer follows
  the canvas's fractional box wherever the page puts it): `<gpui-native-views>` holds a
  `<gpui-native-view-slot>` per view (`gpui::native_view_placement::visible`, `overflow: hidden`, coloured by
  `native_view_placement::background`) around a `<gpui-native-view>` (the view's bounds) around the element. Each
  frame, before the renderer draws, a view whose placement changed gets CSS `transform: translate(..)`, width and
  height: the bounds are whole device pixels already, so both boxes sit on the hole's pixels at any scale; unchanged
  views are not touched, so a frame that places nothing writes no style. A view not painted, or clipped away
  entirely, is `display: none` (an iframe keeps its page).
  Dropping the last handle removes the slot with the element in it (DOM focus inside it goes to GPUI's IME mirror);
  the last view takes the layer and the document listeners with it and gives the canvas its own styles back.
- Input (`NativeViewInput::View`, as macOS): a capture listener on the document decides, before any element sees a
  pointer event, whether the canvas takes the pointer (`pointer-events: auto`) or lets it through to what is under it
  (`none`), from GPUI's own hit test (`hit_test_native_view`), so an uncovered view's element gets trusted events
  (iframe clicks and wheel scrolls, text selection, video controls). Where GPUI's content takes input over a view,
  a transparent shield sits over the element (new `PlatformWindow::on_native_view_occluders`: the hitboxes inserted
  after a view's, clipped to it, asked after each frame that placed views); a press, move or wheel that lands on a
  shield or the layer is GPUI's after all, and a copy goes to the canvas (in a microtask); one that lands on a
  shield where GPUI now hit-tests the view puts every shield back and goes to the element under it. Events on the
  rest of the page are left alone. The table is `native_view_layout::step` (state × event × what the browser hit →
  target, copy). Views that start to show take the pointer, so a touch's first tap reaches the element; a touch
  release hands it back to them; after a frame a still mouse over a view that moved under it gives it to the view.
  The canvas's `pointerleave` is not reported while the pointer is over a view. `set_focused_native_view` focuses
  the element unless focus is already inside it (an `<input>` in it); DOM focus moving into a slot (the document's
  `focusin`, or the window's `blur` when it moves into an iframe, which the parent sees no `focusin` for) focuses
  the view's handle.
  Events listen in a `Phase` (bubble or capture).
- gpui_wgpu: a WebGL2 canvas is premultiplied by its context's default whatever mode wgpu reports (only `Opaque`), so
  0047's alpha log says so there instead of warning that holes show black.

New files `gpui_web/src/native_views.rs` (the DOM shell) and `native_view_layout.rs` (pure: `placement`, `step`;
tested on the host in patch-test.sh's web-pure crate). The slot lifecycle is ported from flutter/flutter
engine/src/flutter/lib/web_ui/lib/src/engine/platform_views/content_manager.dart (`renderContent`,
`_ensureContentCorrectlySized`, `clearPlatformView`), slots.dart (`createPlatformViewSlot`) and embedder.dart
(`_resetAnchor`, `disposeView`) @ 3.50.1 (BSD-3-Clause; third_party/flutter/PORTED.md lists what was dropped).

Tests: `window::native_view::tests::native_view_occluders_are_the_hitboxes_painted_over_it`;
`native_view_layout::tests::placement_of_a_view` and `pointer_target_steps` (one row per state × event). In a browser:
scripts/web-checks.mjs's `native-view` suite on examples/native-view-web (docs/native-view-web.md).

Why: `NativeView::new` failed on the web (xuckless/parallax#1). The window's canvas, its listeners and the frame are
private to gpui_web; the occluders need GPUI's hitboxes (rule 12).

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

